  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: SQL_SELECT
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: SQL_SELECT
  language: 3
  conceptTitle: 'SELECT: Reading Data from a Table'
  symbol: SELECT
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - SQL_01_SELECT
  - HZ_SQL_01_SELECT
  topics:
  - SELECT
  definition: SQL is a language for working with data in a database. Data is stored
    in tables made of columns (headings) and rows (records). SELECT reads data from
    a table.
  howItWorks: Write SELECT, the column or columns you want, then FROM and the table's
    name, and end with a semicolon. SELECT * means "all columns". Without a WHERE
    filter, every row is returned.
  exampleCode: "Table: pets\n id | name  | animal | age\n  1 | Bolt  | Dog    |  
    4\n  2 | Misty | Cat    |   2\n  3 | Nemo  | Fish   |   1\n\nSELECT animal FROM
    pets;"
  exampleExplanation: SELECT animal picks only the animal column, and FROM pets says
    which table to read. No rows are filtered out, so the result is Dog, Cat, Fish.
  tip: The tables in this game are made-up examples - no real database is used.
